{"test_id": "B03-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Your organization uses shared admin accounts with session logging for CII SCADA systems because the legacy HMIs don't support individual authentication. The CISO argues this satisfies CCoP access control requirements through compensating controls. Does this approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Shared admin accounts on CII SCADA systems do not comply with CCoP 2.0 access control requirements, even with session logging as a compensating control. Clause 5.3.1(c) mandates individual accountability for privileged access to CII systems. Shared accounts fundamentally prevent attribution of actions to specific individuals. Session logging is a detective control that records what happened but cannot prevent unauthorized actions or establish who performed them. The appropriate path is to pursue a waiver under Section 11(7) of the Cybersecurity Act, documenting technical constraints and proposing compensating controls.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "Clause 5.3.1(c) requires individual accountability for privileged access", "source": "CCoP 2.0 Section 5.3.1(c)", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Shared accounts prevent individual attribution of actions", "source": "Regulatory interpretation", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Session logging cannot replace individual authentication", "source": "CCoP security principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Compensating controls substitute for MFA", "Session logging satisfies accountability"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent clause allowing shared accounts"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.2.1", "5.3.1(c)"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "insufficient_segmentation", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 2 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "A flat OT network with no segmentation does not comply with CCoP 2.0 requirements. Section 5.5 explicitly requires network segmentation to limit the blast radius of security incidents. Connecting PLCs directly to the corporate network without segmentation exposes critical control systems to threats originating in IT environments. Physical security of the control room does not address cyber threats. The reference to Section 10 (OT-specific requirements) reinforces that OT systems must be segmented from IT networks. A demilitarized zone (DMZ) or proper network segmentation must be implemented to isolate OT systems.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.5.1", "5.5.2", "10.2.1"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["5.5.1", "5.5.2", "10.2.1"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "REJECT"}}
{"test_id": "B03-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 3 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Temporary use of shared accounts does not comply. CCoP 5.2.1 / 5.3.1(c) requires individual accountability regardless of transition status. Use service accounts with proper credentials for each administrator or implement role-based access.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.2.1", "5.3.1(c)"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 4 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Legacy systems without MFA support require a waiver under Section 11(7). Isolation on a separate VLAN is an appropriate compensating control, but does not exempt the organization from pursuing the waiver. The waiver application must document genuine technical infeasibility and include a migration plan.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["section 11"]}}
{"test_id": "B03-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 5 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "8-hour session timeout exceeds reasonable session management for high-consequence systems. CCoP 2.0 §5.1.4 requires automatic session termination after inactivity for a pre-defined time, or compensating monitoring with anomaly alerting where termination is not feasible. ATC systems are high-consequence; the pre-defined timeout must be commensurate with the cybersecurity risk profile, and 8 hours is not justifiable on that basis.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.1.4"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 6 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Quarterly patching may be acceptable with a documented risk assessment and compensating controls. CCoP 5.10 requires systems be kept up to date but allows operational constraints. The organization must document: (1) monthly patching infeasibility, (2) compensating monitoring, (3) isolation from less critical systems.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.10.1", "5.10.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 7 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "The service provider is not directly bound by CCoP, but the CII organization it serves is bound by CCoP §3.8 to flow contractual cybersecurity terms down to it. Per §3.8.3, the CIIO must include terms in the agreement with you that address the cybersecurity of the CII, and per §3.8.4 the CIIO must validate your compliance with those terms. As a result, you should expect (and should be prepared to satisfy) contract clauses that go beyond a standard commercial SLA — e.g., security incident notification, access controls, audit rights, and right-to-audit. Standard SLA alone will not enable the CIIO to meet its §3.8 obligations.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.8.1", "3.8.3", "3.8.4"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-008", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 8 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Cloud provider certifications do not eliminate the CII organization's security obligations. CCoP Section 3.7 applies regardless of hosting model. The agency must implement its own access controls, monitoring, and incident response. Cloud provider security is a shared responsibility model, not a complete delegation.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.7.1", "3.7.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-009", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 9 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 §3.2.1 requires the CIIO to operate a cybersecurity risk-management framework, and §3.2.5 requires risks in the register to be reviewed and monitored regularly. 'Regularly' is a continuous obligation — a 2-year gap with no reassessment is not consistent with §3.2.5, particularly given technology and threat-landscape evolution. The 'no major changes' argument is not a defence: §3.2.5 is not change-triggered. Reassess on a defined cadence (typically annual at minimum, more frequent for high-risk CII) and maintain auditable evidence of regular review.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.2.1", "3.2.5"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-010", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 10 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Outsourced monitoring can satisfy Section 3.8 if internal visibility is maintained. The port must have real-time access to alerts, ability to direct investigation response, and clear SLAs. The MSSP acts as an extension of the CII's security function, not a complete replacement.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.8.1", "3.8.2", "3.8.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-011", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 11 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Regulatory postponement is not a CCoP exemption. Section 11(7) requires a formal waiver application with documented technical constraints. COVID-19 urgency does not automatically exempt compliance. Compensating controls must be implemented during any grace period.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["section 11"]}}
{"test_id": "B03-012", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 12 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Singapore CII operations must comply with CCoP 2.0 regardless of parent bank controls. While parent bank controls may be leveraged, the Singapore entity must demonstrate local compliance including incident reporting to CSA, local risk assessment, and alignment with Singapore-specific requirements.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.1.1", "3.8.1"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 13 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Two knowledge-based factors (password + security question) do not constitute MFA in the conventional sense — both are 'something you know'. CCoP 2.0 §5.3.1(c) mandates multi-factor authentication for privileged access to CII, and §5.1.2 requires authentication controls commensurate with the CII's risk profile. While CCoP 2.0 does not itself enumerate authenticator categories, the prevailing interpretation (per industry standards such as NIST SP 800-63B that CCoP relies on for technical specificity) treats two knowledge factors as a single factor. Implement true MFA combining a knowledge factor with a possession factor (hardware token, mobile authenticator) or an inherence factor (biometric).", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.3.1", "5.1.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 14 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "A shared break-glass account that bypasses MFA is not directly permitted by CCoP 2.0. §5.3.1(c) mandates MFA for privileged access to CII without an explicit emergency carve-out, and §5.3.1(a) requires privileged access be granted only to selected authorised accounts (which a shared break-glass account undermines). Compensating controls (manager approval, logging, post-use audit) are good practice but do not in themselves create a CCoP exemption. If genuine emergency-access infeasibility exists, the proper path is a §1.6 waiver (which invokes Cybersecurity Act s.11(7)) documenting the technical constraint and the compensating controls. Otherwise, replace the shared break-glass account with a named-user emergency-access workflow that retains MFA (e.g., emergency role activation tied to an individual identity).", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.3.1", "1.6.1", "1.6.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 15 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Acquired systems do not receive temporary exemption from CCoP. Upon acquisition, the expanded CII footprint must be brought into compliance. A documented gap closure plan with timeline is required. During integration, compensating controls must be applied to highest-risk systems.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.1.1", "3.3.1"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-016", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 16 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Third-party remote access to CII systems requires enhanced monitoring per CCoP Sections 3.8 and 5.7. ISO 27001 does not exempt the need for: (1) session logging for all remote access, (2) activity monitoring, (3) least privilege access, (4) periodic access review.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["3.8.1", "5.7.1", "5.7.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-017", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 17 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "24-hour log retention does not meet CCoP requirements. CCoP 2.0 §6.1.4(c) explicitly mandates a minimum log retention period of 12 months from the date of the event to which the log relates. 24-hour retention is two orders of magnitude below the required floor and undermines forensic investigation, since attacker dwell time is commonly measured in weeks or months. Implement a log-retention policy that meets or exceeds the §6.1.4(c) 12-month minimum, and ensure logs are also protected against unauthorised access, modification and deletion (§6.1.4(b)) and use a consistent time source (§6.1.4(a)).", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["6.1.4"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-018", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 18 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "CCoP Section 9.2 requires role-specific training. Generic security awareness is insufficient for OT operators who face different threats and work with different systems. Training must be tailored to: (1) OT-specific threats, (2) operational security constraints, (3) emergency response procedures.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "9", "clause_reference": ["9.2.1", "9.2.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-019", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 19 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "CCoP Section 7.3 requires incident response plans be tested regularly. An untested plan cannot be relied upon. Annual testing for CII organizations is minimum. Testing validates: (1) contact information accuracy, (2) response procedures, (3) integration with CSA reporting.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "7", "clause_reference": ["7.3.1", "7.3.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-020", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 20 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Air-gapped systems still require CCoP controls. Physical access vectors exist (insider threats, compromised maintenance laptops, portable media). CCoP applies to all CII systems regardless of connectivity. Air-gap is a defense-in-depth layer, not a complete security solution.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "10", "clause_reference": ["10.2.1", "10.2.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-021", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 21 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "This requires a risk-based decision. CCoP 5.10.1(g) allows compensating controls where a patch cannot be applied. However, warranty preservation may be a valid consideration. The appropriate path: (1) documented risk assessment accepting unpatched state, (2) enhanced isolation, (3) monitoring for exploitation, (4) migration plan.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.10.1(d)", "5.10.1(e)", "5.10.1(g)"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-022", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 22 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "CCoP Section 6.4 requires threat-intelligence-driven security. Sector-specific threat intelligence is a relevant risk factor. Ignoring sector threats until specifically targeted violates the risk-based approach. Enhanced monitoring is appropriate response to credible threat intelligence.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "6", "clause_reference": ["6.4.1", "6.4.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-023", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 23 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Permanent MFA exemptions for executives do not comply with CCoP 5.3.1. MFA is mandatory for CII systems. Executive convenience is not a valid exemption criterion. If technical barriers genuinely exist, a Section 11(7) waiver is required. Usability complaints do not justify exemption.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.3.1(c)"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["section 11"]}}
{"test_id": "B03-024", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 24 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "CCoP Section 8.1.4 requires backup systems be tested to confirm restoration capability. Documentation alone is insufficient. Testing can be planned during scheduled maintenance to minimize disruption. Untested backups cannot be relied upon during emergencies.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "8", "clause_reference": ["8.1.4"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-025", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 25 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Internal penetration testing can contribute to compliance, but the CCoP text does not support the blanket claim that external testing is always mandatory. The grounded requirements are that penetration testing must be conducted at the cadence required by §5.15.1, additional testing is required after major system changes under §5.15.2, and any third-party penetration testing service providers used must satisfy the accreditation and certification requirements in §5.15.3. The CIIO should therefore ensure that its chosen testing model is competent, properly scoped and consistent with those clauses rather than assume that external testing is categorically required in every case.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "5", "clause_reference": ["5.15.1", "5.15.2", "5.15.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-026", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 26 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 §4.1.1 requires the CIIO to identify all CII assets and maintain an inventory covering owner/operator, name/description, critical functions, key responsible persons, etc. §4.1.2 requires the inventory be updated 'whenever there is any change to any CII asset or to the information to be recorded'. A 2-year-old inventory cannot meet §4.1.2 in any realistic operating environment, since changes (VMs, cloud deployments, software updates, configuration changes) routinely occur. The 'no major systems added' argument fails because §4.1.2 is change-driven, not threshold-driven.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "4", "clause_reference": ["4.1.1", "4.1.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-027", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 27 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "CCoP Section 3.3 requires documented policies and procedures. Technical controls without policy documentation are not sufficient for compliance. Policies provide: (1) control consistency, (2) staff guidance, (3) audit evidence, (4) continuity when staff change.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.3.1", "3.3.3"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-028", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 28 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "A post-incident control review is necessary but not sufficient to satisfy CCoP 2.0's ongoing review obligations. §3.2.5 requires that risks in the register be reviewed and monitored regularly, and §2.1.1 governs how audit findings are remediated. 'Regularly' is a continuous, threat-landscape-aware obligation; it does not pause for 18 months because of a recent incident-driven hardening. Maintain the regular review cadence and document how post-incident enhancements feed into the risk register and the CIIO's broader compliance posture.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "3", "clause_reference": ["3.2.5"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["2.1.1"]}}
{"test_id": "B03-029", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 29 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "CCoP specifies security outcomes (what must be achieved) not specific tools (how to achieve). Open-source tools can satisfy CCoP requirements if they provide equivalent capability. The organization must demonstrate: (1) tool effectiveness, (2) appropriate coverage, (3) maintenance and update processes.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.4.1", "5.1.2"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null}}
{"test_id": "B03-030", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B03", "input": {"question": "Conditional compliance scenario 30 for CII organization in Singapore. Does the proposed approach comply with CCoP 2.0?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"expected_label": "non-compliant", "expected_response": "Complying with stricter CAAS requirements may satisfy some overlapping control objectives, but it does not automatically satisfy CCoP 2.0. The CIIO must separately ensure that the designated CII complies with the applicable CCoP requirements and separately comply with CAAS requirements. The grounded answer is to map overlap control by control rather than assume that one regime fully replaces the other.", "key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP 2.0 requirements are mandatory for CII organizations", "source": "CCoP 2.0", "tier": "critical"}, {"fact": "Compliance requires meeting all specified controls", "source": "CCoP 2.0 principles", "tier": "important"}]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Proposed approach satisfies CCoP without waiver", "Operational convenience justifies non-compliance"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Non-existent exemption for described scenario"]}, "metadata": {"section": "1", "clause_reference": ["1.4.1", "1.4.5", "1.5.1"], "domain": "OT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "conditional_compliance", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "negative", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["1.4.1", "1.4.5", "1.5.1"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "PASS_WITH_QUALIFICATION"}}
